ase type versus being used as a generic category label.\n\nChecking example 2 though, it doesn't fit as neatly since \"cancer\" stands alone rather than as part of a gene/protein compound. Maybe the unifying trigger is broader: the model fails when a disease term appears as a bare modifier lacking a site-specific qualifier, whether that's because it's embedded in a longer biological entity name or because it's an unspecified category term like \"cancer\" used generically. I need to settle on a single pattern insight that captures this primary mechanism while accounting for the secondary case.\n\nSo a disease mention nested inside or modifying a non-disease named entity (like a gene or protein) gets suppressed as a Modifier rather than tagged as the entity itself. And for \"cancer cases,\" maybe the LLM treats bare generic disease heads without a specific site qualifier as too non-specific to count, unlike the specific \"ovarian cancer\" in true positives.\n\nThat feature fails too—\"breast or ovarian cancer\" isn't annotated consistently in the TP list, while other instances of \"ovarian cancer\" appear elsewhere as annotated spans. The gold annotations themselves seem incomplete or inconsistent here, so I shouldn't rely too heavily on this particular example to derive the distinguishing feature.\n\nThe real pattern emerging is this: the disease term gets nested as an attributive modifier inside a larger compound entity name (often gene/protein-headed, or a bare \"cancer\" modifying \"cases\"), and the model absorbs the whole string into a non-schema label rather than isolating the disease term. In contrast, true positives have the disease term functioning as a self-contained modifier phrase, like \"ovarian cancer population\" or \"ovarian cancer risk.\"\n\nI'm double-checking one edge case—a \"DTD gene\" instance marked Modifier in what looks like a control example—to see whether the model handles abbreviation-based pre-modifiers correctly, since that could either 