, 3, and 4 fit this pattern. Example 2 does not, because \"91 Caucasian cancer cases\" is a cohort descriptor, not a nomenclature label.\n\n**Hypothesis Check:** The nomenclature trigger explains 3 of 4 cases. To cover Example 2, I refined the trigger to include a generic, unqualified disease category term (\"cancer\") used attributively. The model likely fails to treat a bare category word as a disease mention. \"Ovarian cancer\" in the true positives is specific, which is one reason it is recognized.\n\n## Step 2: Pattern Insight\n\n- **Pattern Name:** Nested Disease Span Inside a Non-Disease Label (Gene/Protein Nomenclature and Bare Category Modifiers)\n- **Confusion Trigger:** A disease-denoting span is embedded as a pre-head constituent of a larger noun phrase. Two variants occur:\n  1. The phrase is official gene/protein nomenclature whose name contains a disease string (e.g., \"breast cancer susceptibility gene 1\", \"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein\"). The model absorbs the whole string into one gene/protein identifier and emits O.\n  2. A generic, unqualified category term (\"cancer\") modifies a cohort noun (\"Caucasian cancer cases\"). The model does not recognize it as a disease mention.\n- **Contrastive Evidence:** The true positives keep the disease span visible and separate from any nomenclature wrapper. \"Ovarian cancer\" is site-specific and modifies epidemiological nouns (cases, population, risk). \"DTD gene\" and \"SLS genes\" use short disease tokens before the generic noun \"gene,\" with no nomenclature string around them. All of these are labelled Modifier.\n- **Linguistic Rule (Proposed):** When a disease-denoting span (full name, abbreviation, or category noun such as \"cancer\") appears pre-nominally inside a larger noun phrase, label that span **Modifier**, even if the phrase head is a gene, protein, or cohort noun. Do not absorb the span into a gene/protein O-label. If the disease string is part of official nomenclature (e.g., \"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome protein\"), it does not refer to a disease entity, so it should be Modifier rather than SpecificDisease. A bare \"cancer\" before \"cases\" should also be Modifier.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 4327, "output_tokens": 9050, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 7887}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}